   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Janis Peksa and Luigi Bianchi</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Reproducible EEG-BCI Experiments: Frame-Confirmed Timing and Physical SSVEP Validation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Reproducible EEG-BCI Experiments: Frame-Confirmed Timing and Physical SSVEP Validation</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>brain--computer interface; EEG; SSVEP; reproducibility; stimulus timing; experimental provenance</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Reproducible EEG-based brain--computer interface (BCI) experiments require traceability from the configured protocol through runtime execution to acquisition context and</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Reproducible EEG-based brain--computer interface (BCI) experiments require traceability from the configured protocol through runtime execution to acquisition context and</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">where relevant</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">where relevant</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the physical stimulus delivered to the participant. This study presents and evaluates a workflow that links machine-readable SSVEP protocols</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the physical stimulus delivered to the participant. This study presents and evaluates a workflow that links machine-readable SSVEP protocols</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">deterministic session planning</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">deterministic session planning</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">frame-confirmed events</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">frame-confirmed events</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">raw acquisition provenance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">raw acquisition provenance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">post-session integrity checks</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">post-session integrity checks</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and independent photoresistor-based display validation. The contribution is not a new acquisition platform or timing sensor</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and independent photoresistor-based display validation. The contribution is not a new acquisition platform or timing sensor</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but the integration of these evidence layers into a single auditable experimental chain and the empirical assessment of their consistency. All 61 automated tests passed for the frozen software release. Three optical sweeps covering 8--15 Hz yielded 48 valid measurements</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but the integration of these evidence layers into a single auditable experimental chain and the empirical assessment of their consistency. All 61 automated tests passed for the frozen software release. Three optical sweeps covering 8--15 Hz yielded 48 valid measurements</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">all within the predefined 0.25 Hz criterion. The mean absolute error was 0.0345 Hz</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">all within the predefined 0.25 Hz criterion. The mean absolute error was 0.0345 Hz</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and the maximum was 0.0668 Hz. In a separate 12.75 Hz rendering-load experiment</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and the maximum was 0.0668 Hz. In a separate 12.75 Hz rendering-load experiment</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">all 12 measurements also passed</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">all 12 measurements also passed</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">with mean absolute errors of 0.0170 Hz for a single stimulus and 0.0154 Hz with three simultaneous distractors. Local optical pulse-width anomalies were</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">with mean absolute errors of 0.0170 Hz for a single stimulus and 0.0154 Hz with three simultaneous distractors. Local optical pulse-width anomalies were</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">nevertheless</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">nevertheless</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">observed</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">observed</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">particularly at 13--14 Hz and in two multi-stimulus repetitions</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">particularly at 13--14 Hz and in two multi-stimulus repetitions</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">showing that dominant-frequency agreement and local timing regularity are distinct validation properties. Formal empirical validation targeted software and session integrity and physical display output; it did not quantify end-to-end event-to-EEG latency through the OpenBCI transport path. The results support a more explicit separation of configured intent</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">showing that dominant-frequency agreement and local timing regularity are distinct validation properties. Formal empirical validation targeted software and session integrity and physical display output; it did not quantify end-to-end event-to-EEG latency through the OpenBCI transport path. The results support a more explicit separation of configured intent</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">runtime timing evidence</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">runtime timing evidence</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">acquisition provenance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">acquisition provenance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and physical stimulus verification in reproducible EEG-BCI experimentation.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and physical stimulus verification in reproducible EEG-BCI experimentation.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
